A||||4||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||Iron Duke, pulley removal:  freeing the crank bolt||||5||
Z||000000||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||04-22-2001||07:39 PM||||4cyl 2.5L Iron duke question:<BR>How is the bolt on the pulley end of the crankshaft removed?<P>It's counter-threaded right?  (meaning that it loosens by turning clockwise)<P>My buddy and I could not get the bolt to budge (300 ft/lb impact gun).  <P>What's the best way to get this bolt loose?  (the engine is bolted to an engine stand now)||||reg||5
Z||000001||Rodrv6||04-22-2001||09:11 PM||||Well, it should be good and tight now <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>The bolt is standard thread--in other words it turns counterclockwise to loosen it!<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000002||84Bill||04-22-2001||10:24 PM||||Ahh yes the old German torque method... "Goodentight"||||reg||10
Z||000003||MrPBody||04-23-2001||07:08 PM||||Dunno.  Mine wasn't that tight; I removed it with the engine in the car.  Maybe a previous mechanic installed yours with red Loctite.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||04-24-2001||02:11 AM||||I got the bolt out.  WD-40 and time seems to be the magic combination.<P>After four hours of soaking it, the bolt just came free.  It was kind of stupid, really.  I just walked out to the garage and though "what the hell"  (or something like that) and hit it with the impact gun.<P>I was shocked when it just came off with a beautiful "Vft . . ." of my relatively wimpy impact gun.<P>Tonight, I broke a head bolt (that damn one on the exhuast side in the middle).  So the engine is hanging by it's head on the engine stand about 1" off the ground.<P>I got this idea from another post I saw on the Forum a few weeks ago.  It does seem to be working, but that head is being kinda' stubborn.  Oh well, that's a subject for another post.<P>Let's all take a moment and praise the magic of WD-40 . . . it's a Fiero's best friend!||||reg||1
